---
title: "Hybrid retrieval"
description: "Combine dense and sparse candidate generation with RRF or DBSF in QQL."
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

Hybrid retrieval combines semantic dense similarity with lexical sparse
matching. QQL exposes a shorthand and an explicit multi-stage form.

## Shorthand

{% qqlExample title="Schema-resolved hybrid search" %}
```sql
QUERY TEXT 'zero downtime deployment' FROM runbooks
USING HYBRID DENSE dense SPARSE bm25 FUSION RRF
WHERE service = 'gateway'
LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

If the collection has exactly one dense and one sparse vector, omit the names:

{% qqlExample title="Hybrid defaults" %}
```sql
QUERY 'zero downtime deployment' FROM runbooks
USING HYBRID
LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

## Explicit candidate stages

{% qqlExample title="Weighted RRF pipeline" %}
```sql
WITH
  semantic AS (
    QUERY TEXT 'zero downtime deployment'
    USING dense
    WHERE service = 'gateway'
    LIMIT 120
  ),
  lexical AS (
    QUERY TEXT 'zero downtime deployment'
    USING bm25
    WHERE service = 'gateway'
    LIMIT 180
  )
QUERY FUSION RRF
FROM runbooks
PREFETCH (semantic, lexical)
PARAMS (rrf_k = 40, rrf_weights = [0.7, 0.3])
LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

Use the explicit form when candidate limits, filters, or weights differ by
stage. `rrf_weights` length must equal the prefetch count.

## RRF or DBSF?

| Fusion | Prefer when |
|---|---|
| RRF | Ranking positions are reliable but score scales differ |
| DBSF | Normalized score magnitude carries useful information |
